  "Number": 17,
  "Question": "The sides of a triangle have lengths \\(6.5,\\) \\(10,\\) and \\(s,\\) where \\(s\\) is a whole number. What is the smallest possible value of \\(s?\\)",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(3\\)",
    "B": "\\(4\\)",
    "C": "\\(5\\)",
    "D": "\\(6\\)",
    "E": "\\(7\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Solution": "In a triangle, any two side lengths must add to more than the third. Consequently, the third side must be strictly greater than the difference of the other two lengths and strictly less than their sum:\n\\[\n10-6.5<s<10+6.5,\n\\]\nor\n\\[\n3.5<s<16.5.\n\\]\nThe smallest whole number greater than \\(3.5\\) is \\(4\\), and it also satisfies the upper bound. Thus the smallest possible value is \\(\\boxed{4}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{B}}\\).",
